{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n39 \n \n \nD. Status of Data Analysis \nThe raw non-imaging data acquired from round 1 of Flame Design testing (e.g., gas flow \nrates, burner surface temperature, photomultiplier tube measurements of flame \nemissions, radiometer measurements of flame thermal emission, far-field gas \ntemperatures, and pressures) has been converted to engineering units . All of the raw \n(hobj) images obtained from the ACME camera have been successfully converted to tiff \nfiles for further analysis.  Thin filament pyrometry is being used to obtain fiber \ntemperature measurements in ACME camera images that have SiC fibers inserted in the \nflame.  Soot temperature measurements using two-color ratio pyrometry are being \nacquired from ACME camera images that have soot and no fibers.  Burn time and \nextinction diameter have been acquired for radiatively extinguishing flames using the \nphotomultiplier tubes and analog camera, respectively.  HiBMs camera images are being \nexamined for use in calculating the soot volume fraction.  Intensified camera images have \nyet to be analyzed. \n \nA one-dimensional computational model is being implemented to assist in the \ninvestigation of spherical diffusion flames in microgravity.  Also, collaborations are \nunderway with scientists at the Russian Academy of Sciences for further simulations.  The \nRussian team is focusing on detailed chemistry and more realistic boundary conditions, \nwhile the US team is focusing on a general understanding of the flame dynamics. \n \nE. Preliminary Research Findings \nFlame Design results have shown that spherical diffusion flames in microgravity have \nunique characteristics that are valuable for fundamental studies of diffusion flames. \nFlame size increases with reactant flow rate and decreases with ambient oxygen \nconcentration.  Radiative heat loss scales with flame size such that very small flames - just \nabove their kinetic extinction limits - have low radiative loss, and asymptotically approach \nsteady-state behavior.  Larger flames increase in size over time until the rate of radiative \nheat loss exceeds the rate of chemical heat release, and the flame extinguishes via \nradiative extinction.  Before total extinction occurs in these flames, they experience an \nunstable, oscillatory mode, in which they partially extinguish and reform.  The oscillations \ngrow in magnitude until total extinction.  For constant ambient oxygen concentration,", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 39}}